WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:09.930
Uruguay is a country famous for soccer.

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.900
As we celebrate Russia’s World Cup this
summer, we’ve checked the

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:16.960
hall of fame and there are only 8 countries
that have ever won a final.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:18.180
Uruguay, a really small country

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.540
with little more than 3 million inhabitants is one of them.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.060
They even won the cup twice and in both cases

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:30.800
they beat their neighbours: Brazil and Argentina.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.610
So in today’s video, we’re gonna talk
about how Uruguay has beaten its neighbours

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:36.989
but not in soccer but

00:00:36.989 --> 00:00:38.080
in economics instead.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:41.770
Yes, my dear viewers, alongside Chile, Uruguay
is the wealthiest country in South America

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:43.679
GDP per capita

00:00:43.679 --> 00:00:44.679
wise.

00:00:44.679 --> 00:00:47.710
It’s also one of the most stable when it
comes to politics and has some of the best

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:48.710
public

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:49.710
services.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:50.769
But there is even more to this.

00:00:50.769 --> 00:00:53.949
We can actually say that Uruguay is the model
for the entire

00:00:53.949 --> 00:00:55.489
South American left wing.

00:00:55.489 --> 00:00:58.260
Have you ever heard about their former president,
JOSE MUJICA?

00:00:58.260 --> 00:00:59.329
Known as the

00:00:59.329 --> 00:01:03.320
‘World’s poorest president’, he quickly
became the ultimate left wing rockstar.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:04.320
The only world leader who

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:08.930
refused to live in the presidential palace
and kept driving an old Volkswagen instead

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:09.930
of an expensive

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:11.380
official car.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:18.890
“From a life philosophy perspective, I believe
it’s better to live with little luggage.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:19.890
Just the minimum

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:20.890
necessary.

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:22.049
No material complexity.

00:01:22.049 --> 00:01:25.979
If you have too many complexities… you end
up spending too much time.

00:01:25.979 --> 00:01:30.490
Nonetheless, while Mujica had this simple
lifestyle, his country grew economically,

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:31.490
attracted foreign

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.909
companies and became a pioneer in social Rights.

00:01:33.909 --> 00:01:36.330
With Mujica as president, Uruguay passed a
bill for the

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:38.500
legalization of same-sex marriage.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:42.540
But, undoubtedly, his most famous policy was
the legalization of

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.840
recreational marijuana sales.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:48.299
Way before California or any other place,
Uruguay was the first country to

00:01:48.299 --> 00:01:51.960
legalize weed selling and consumption.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.290
And the country’s progress doesn’t stop
there.

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:56.899
If you take a static picture of Uruguay, you
will see a

00:01:56.899 --> 00:02:11.350
country that, arguably, is the best place
to live in all of South America.

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:12.770
Unlike Brazil and Argentina,

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:15.890
Uruguay’s economy hasn’t stopped growing
since 2002.

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:18.750
They have a thriving middle class and the
highest

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:20.760
wages in the region, along with Chile.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:24.200
But wait a minute because not everything is
a field of roses!

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.161
In fact, as such a prosperous country, you

00:02:26.161 --> 00:02:29.190
might expect it to have lots of immigrants,
right?

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:31.150
The reality is way different.

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:32.150
And this is not because

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:33.910
they have strong immigration laws.

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:37.950
People just don’t pick Uruguay as a country
of residence because…

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.910
well, things are starting to go sour here.

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:44.730
They have around an 8% unemployment rate and
their economy is

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:48.690
reducing their growth speed while other countries
like PERU and CHILE, seem to offer opportunities

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:49.690
for

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:50.690
everyone.

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:51.690
So today’s question is…

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.900
Why has Uruguay’s economy worked out so
well until now?

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.010
And what are the current

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:56.010
problems they’re facing?

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:57.870
Today we are going to answer to all of these
questions.

00:02:57.870 --> 00:02:59.190
But before we do, let’s

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:02.060
take a look back at history.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:04.400
AMERICAS’ SWITZERLAND

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:09.230
Uruguay was born in 1830 after a war between
BRAZIL and ARGENTINA.

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:10.540
The British were to negotiate the

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:15.840
peace agreements and they badly wanted Uruguay
to be an independent country.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:17.240
Why?

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:20.040
Well, Montevideo’s

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:25.800
harbour was a jewel and they didn’t want
it to end up in Brazilian or Argentinian hands.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.630
Back then, Uruguay was almost uninhabited.

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:30.950
It was inhabited by a tribe, the so called
CHARRUAS *CZECH

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:32.700
PRONOUNCE: ČARUAS* but they were soon exterminated.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:35.180
This is why, unlike any other South American
country,

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:40.010
most of the Uruguayans have a European ethnicity,
mainly Spanish and Italian roots.

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:44.431
In those times, if you were an ambitious boy
looking for a career and opportunities to

00:03:44.431 --> 00:03:45.431
make money,

00:03:45.431 --> 00:03:47.400
Uruguay was the place to go.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.780
Better than America or Australia.

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:51.810
It was a modern democracy with rule of law

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:53.700
and a thriving economy.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:04:01.320
Until the 60s of the 20th century, this country
was among the wealthiest.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:03.270
Way richer than many European

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:04.270
nations.

00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:05.590
So now you might wonder…

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:06.590
What happened?

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:09.160
Because, despite being prosperous among the
South American

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:14.410
nations, Uruguay is still way behind any European
country, let alone America.

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:15.490
So what happened to make

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:18.310
them stop growing that dramatically?

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:21.910
The answer lies in… the EUROPEAN UNION!

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:22.990
Yes, you heard me right!

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:24.650
Listen, my dear viewers, if you thought

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:28.990
this idea of a trade war was Donald Trump’s
invention… you are very mistaken.

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:32.979
In the year 1958, the European Single Market
was created.

00:04:32.979 --> 00:04:34.729
And one of the first things they did was

00:04:34.729 --> 00:04:40.820
protect their agriculture from Uruguayan meat.

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:43.550
This meant that Uruguay lost one of its biggest
customers.

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:46.479
And America didn’t want to trade with them
either.

00:04:46.479 --> 00:04:48.650
In the middle of the cold war, they were too
busy

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:52.650
dealing with bigger problems than this little
country.

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:55.449
So day by day, Uruguay became stagnant.

00:04:55.449 --> 00:04:59.120
And the government in Montevideo, the country’s
capital, didn’t

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:03.089
have any other alternative than looking to
their neighbours: Argentina.

00:05:03.089 --> 00:05:04.300
All of a sudden, Uruguay and

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:09.560
Argentina became something like the ROBERT
RODRIGUEZ and QUENTIN TARANTINO of South America.

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:10.560
Did

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:11.560
Argentina have a crisis?

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:12.639
So did Uruguay!

00:05:12.639 --> 00:05:14.320
Did Argentina have a military dictatorship?

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:15.660
So did Uruguay!

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:16.660
Did

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:18.639
Argentina have a democracy?

00:05:18.639 --> 00:05:20.620
So did Uruguay!

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:24.680
So my dear viewers… what happened in Argentina
in the year 2002?

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:25.680
Exactly!

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:27.300
They had the biggest financial

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:29.460
crisis in history!

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.229
The infamous ‘CORRALITO’.

00:05:31.229 --> 00:05:35.970
And in that very same year, Uruguay’s GDP
fell by 7.7%.

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:36.970
A

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:41.860
big time catastrophe.

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:45.550
And this was when Uruguay realized that it
had to find new trade Partners.

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:46.550
But how

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:48.270
do you do that?

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:50.410
We’ll see right now!

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:53.469
THE 0 KM OF SOUTH AMERICA

00:05:53.469 --> 00:05:56.360
During the 80s, Uruguay returned to democracy.

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:58.669
And the government did something really, really,

00:05:58.669 --> 00:06:02.229
interesting: they created the so called ZONAS
FRANCAS.

00:06:02.229 --> 00:06:04.139
These were special territories inside of the

00:06:04.139 --> 00:06:09.120
country where companies were free to not pay
taxes: no VAT, no income tax, no corporate

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:10.120
tax.

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:11.120
The only

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:13.819
request was that 75% of the jobs be given
to the locals.

00:06:13.819 --> 00:06:15.340
Does that remind you of something?

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:16.340
Exactly!

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:17.340
This

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:19.680
is the same as the special economic zones
that the Chinese have and that we like so

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.499
much here at

00:06:21.499 --> 00:06:22.749
VisualPolitik.

00:06:22.749 --> 00:06:27.090
Today, Uruguay has 11 tax free zones.

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:33.120
And there are three different kinds.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:34.669
First, we have those oriented

00:06:34.669 --> 00:06:37.050
to logistics and international trade.

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:39.190
After that, we have the zones that are designed
for attracting

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:43.889
multinational corporations so they can use
them as the headquarters for their South American

00:06:43.889 --> 00:06:46.090
operations.

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:51.460
Finally, we have the areas that are meant
to have high tech industry.

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:53.139
This is why Uruguay is a regional

00:06:53.139 --> 00:06:56.580
leader in things like Pharma research and
software.

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:58.620
But this is not the end of the story!

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:01.650
These tax free zones have a unique advantage.

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:02.650
Something that makes

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:04.150
Uruguay a model for other countries.

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:08.059
Yes, dear viewers, we are talking about POLITICAL
stability.

00:07:08.059 --> 00:07:09.059
Do you

00:07:09.059 --> 00:07:10.059
think I’m kidding?

00:07:10.059 --> 00:07:11.059
No way!

00:07:11.059 --> 00:07:14.629
In the year 2005, Uruguay changed its government
leadership and they had, at last, a left wing

00:07:14.629 --> 00:07:15.629
president.

00:07:15.629 --> 00:07:17.990
That would be, of course, TABARE VAZQUEZ,
from the OPEN FRONT party.

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:20.110
You see, in most South American

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:23.889
countries, having such an ideological shift
in the government means that most of the work

00:07:23.889 --> 00:07:24.889
made by the

00:07:24.889 --> 00:07:33.069
previous administrations goes into the trash.

00:07:33.069 --> 00:07:35.930
So you might think that, under a brand new
left government,

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:38.689
those tax free zones would no longer exist.

00:07:38.689 --> 00:07:39.689
Right?

00:07:39.689 --> 00:07:42.360
Well, the reality couldn’t be more different.

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:43.360
And this

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:47.650
is the great thing about this country: the
bipartisan consensus.

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:50.699
This is why, between 2005 and 2014, the

00:07:50.699 --> 00:07:56.180
accumulated investment in these tax free zones
rose to almost 6000 million dollars.

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:57.180
And all of this

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:02.349
happened while, first, TABARE VAZQUEZ and
then JOSE MUJICA, managed the country.

00:08:02.349 --> 00:08:06.289
“People are not only looking for profit.

00:08:06.289 --> 00:08:07.979
They also want safety.

00:08:07.979 --> 00:08:09.550
And this is what Uruguay has to offer.

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:10.550
It

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:12.020
is not a matter of this or the other Government.

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:14.669
This is a genuine Uruguayan attitude.

00:08:14.669 --> 00:08:18.659
And now you might wonder…

00:08:18.659 --> 00:08:21.349
What does Uruguay get from these tax free
zones?

00:08:21.349 --> 00:08:23.090
Well, my dear viewers… there

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:24.789
is a double benefit for them.

00:08:24.789 --> 00:08:28.939
On one hand, they are able to appeal to industries
that otherwise would set

00:08:28.939 --> 00:08:30.969
up in other countries with bigger markets.

00:08:30.969 --> 00:08:33.360
And these foreign companies need providers.

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:34.360
This is why,

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:37.969
sooner than expected, many small enterprises
are created around the country.

00:08:37.969 --> 00:08:39.640
And, of course, they require

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:42.640
highly skilled labour and good salaries.

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:46.000
This is why Uruguayans don’t need to emigrate
in order to find a

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:47.450
good job.

00:08:47.450 --> 00:08:52.050
But wait a moment here!

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:56.070
Because we said that Uruguay needed to change
their trade partners, right?

00:08:56.070 --> 00:08:57.070
DO you

00:08:57.070 --> 00:09:01.410
remember how we said that they could no longer
depend on Argentina or Brazil for their exports.

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:02.410
So you

00:09:02.410 --> 00:09:06.140
might wonder… what do the tax free zones
have to do with all of this?

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:07.520
Well… think about it.

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:08.520
Thanks to

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:12.420
these innovative industries that set up in
the country, Uruguay could stop relying on

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:13.420
the commodity

00:09:13.420 --> 00:09:14.420
sector.

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:16.710
Prior to this, their economy was based mainly
on Soy and Meat.

00:09:16.710 --> 00:09:18.880
Now, they can create more added

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:20.920
value products.

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:26.460
Want an example?

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:31.760
All of the syrup needed in PEPSI drinks sold
all across South America is created in

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:32.760
Uruguay.

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:35.840
In fact, there is a popular drink in Japan
called PEPSI AZUKI.

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:36.840
This variety is made with a plant

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:37.900
called SWEET BEAN.

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:43.000
And, again, the syrup for this beverage is
produced 200km away from Montevideo.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:44.000
In

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:47.770
total, Pepsi represents 7% of everything the
country exports.

00:09:47.770 --> 00:09:48.830
And this is not the only example.

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:51.880
For example, one of the biggest paper making
companies on the planet, a

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:56.460
Finnish company called UPM, has one of their
main factories in Uruguay.

00:09:56.460 --> 00:09:57.660
And, they have already announced

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:03.380
the construction of a brand new facility that
might represent a whole 2.5% extra GDP for

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:04.380
the country.

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:10.290
And if that isn’t enough, Uruguay is also
a software leader.

00:10:10.290 --> 00:10:16.790
Eight Uruguay software developers, among the
best in all of Latin America

00:10:16.790 --> 00:10:21.850
In summary, 30% of Uruguay’s exports are
produced from these tax free zones.

00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:23.850
And guess who is the main

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:24.850
buyer!

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:26.020
Let me give you a clue!

00:10:26.020 --> 00:10:29.620
It starts with CHI and ends with NA.

00:10:29.620 --> 00:10:31.130
Nowadays, Brazil and Argentina

00:10:31.130 --> 00:10:34.170
receive just 20% of this country’s exports.

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:36.540
Another good friend of Uruguay is the Netherlands.

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:39.040
The Rotterdam harbour is the gateway to Europe.

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:40.040
And

00:10:40.040 --> 00:10:41.580
many of the Uruguayan exports go that way.

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:44.070
TO make a comparison, Chile’s economy, despite
being the

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:47.850
wealthiest in the region, is heavily reliant
on their copper mining.

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:49.880
Half of their exports are just this

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:50.880
commodity.

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:55.960
Uruguay, instead, has a very diversified economy:
we talked about software, paper, drinks and,

00:10:55.960 --> 00:10:57.940
of course, soy and meat.

00:10:57.940 --> 00:11:01.680
But if you thought everything is all sunshine
and flowers… then wait a minute!

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:10.460
Because things are not as perfect as you might
think.

00:11:10.460 --> 00:11:11.960
PARTY vs GOVERNMENT

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:17.190
As we said before, since the year 2005, Uruguay
has a Left wing government.

00:11:17.190 --> 00:11:18.190
More specifically, this

00:11:18.190 --> 00:11:21.160
country has been managed by the OPEN FRONT
Party.

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:25.390
And… one thing is true, they are worthy
of that name,

00:11:25.390 --> 00:11:30.370
because it is really an open party, with lots
of different ideologies inside.

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:31.370
This is why, despite what

00:11:31.370 --> 00:11:35.440
it looks like, arguments are a common thing
in the party.

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:41.500
So, on one hand, despite the different presidents,
Uruguay has had the same finance minister

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:42.500
for the last

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:43.500
13 years.

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:48.840
His name is DANILO ASTORI, and you can say
he is one of the architects of this Uruguayan

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:50.100
stability.

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:56.940
But on the other hand, the OPEN FRONT is an
open battlefront between the radical wing

00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:57.940
and the moderate

00:11:57.940 --> 00:11:59.960
wing championed by TABARE VAZQUEZ.

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:03.930
JOSE MUJICA, the left wing rockstar, is somewhere
in the middle.

00:12:03.930 --> 00:12:08.300
And this is why, anytime Uruguay´s government
wants to sign a free trade deal with any other

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:09.300
country,

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:11.380
they must face their own party opposition.

00:12:11.380 --> 00:12:14.060
It happened during the first Vazquez administration,
when they

00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:16.500
tried to sign a trade deal with the United
States.

00:12:16.500 --> 00:12:18.190
It happened when they tried to sign a trade
deal with

00:12:18.190 --> 00:12:19.190
Chile.

00:12:19.190 --> 00:12:22.440
And it might happen now when they wanna sign
a trade deal with China.

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:29.740
“This year, 2018, will mark 30 years of
diplomatic relations with China and this is

00:12:29.740 --> 00:12:30.870
why we have put this

